  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lowy situates Benjamin within a network of early-twentieth-century radical Jewish thinkers in Central Europe, arguing that his thought is best understood as a synthesis of Jewish messianism, German Romantic critique of modernity, and revolutionary Marxism. Benjamin's historical materialism is distinctive precisely because it incorporates the messianic critique of progress as normative teleology; this makes him the first Marxist to genuinely break with the progressive view of history.

  RELEVANCE: This encyclopedia entry provides concise statement of Lowy's interpretation of Benjamin's philosophy of history and its Jewish-Romantic sources, useful as a secondary reference for the review's treatment of Benjamin's relationship to messianism and his critique of social-democratic Marxism.

  POSITION: Jewish-Romantic-Marxist synthesis reading; closely related to Lowy's Fire Alarm.
